it's hard to miss daily reminders of the  globalized marketplace we live in  with the goods and services we use  coming from the four corners of the  world  but the playing field isn't quite so  level as it may appear  or as many here in europe would wish for  particularly when it comes to  multi-billion euros state procurement  contracts  according to parliament european  companies could be losing billions of  euros because access to major services  urban and national infrastructure  projects is blocked  but the mep steering new legislation to  redress that through parliament  says that although europe's markets are  open there's plenty of room for more  foreign  but we're still in the belly of an  economic recession and not everyone  agrees with flinging open the doors of  europe's markets to outsiders  the proposals call for more leverage for  europe on market access  for powers to investigate and follow up  restrictive practices in a relevant  country  and to block market access in europe to  companies from that country if necessary  the proposal passed the first committee  stage comfortably and will go to plenary  for a first reading
 

